WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:07.590
Hello, everybody.

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:09.630
My name is Greg Swinehart.

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:12.730
I have worked at
RStudio for a long time.

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:17.080
This is going to be a different
kind of talk for rstudio::conf,

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:19.810
because I'm not
a data scientist.

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:21.370
I'm a graphic designer.

00:00:21.370 --> 00:00:24.170
Like I graduated in
the social sciences.

00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:26.520
So why on earth am I
up here to talk to you?

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:30.340
I'm asking myself the
exact same question.

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:30.860
Let's see,

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:32.652
can we get my slides
up here on the screen?

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:36.840
Here we go.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:40.540
So I started, I think, the
first design I did for RStudio,

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:44.530
I think three people
work there at the time.

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:47.100
And I joined full time
about 4 and 1/2 years ago.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:49.050
I made this back in
the day actually,

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:51.510
and this started out as
a shirt a couple of years

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:53.382
before hexes were even a thing.

00:00:53.382 --> 00:00:54.840
Joe reached out
and said, I kind of

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:56.910
want to make a baseball
shirt with this thing

00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:57.905
I've been working on.

00:00:57.905 --> 00:00:59.280
Can you make us
a shirt that says

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:00.670
Shiny and kind of baseball font?

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:03.480
And then when hexes came
around, we controversially

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:06.600
went with the baseball font
where all the other hexes

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:07.740
had a different font.

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:10.410
It was very it was there were
some tense conversations,

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:11.500
but we got it.

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:15.150
Now it's no big deal, of course.

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:17.940
So a few months ago, I
moved over to the Shiny team

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:19.890
to see what you all
have been working on

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:23.580
and see if maybe we could update
some things in the process.

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:25.530
What we're going
to talk about today

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:28.380
is kind of what I've seen
in the last couple of months

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:32.220
and what we've begun
to work on to build

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:34.478
some new functionality
for you, to make

00:01:34.478 --> 00:01:35.770
your lives a little bit easier.

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:38.460
So I'm going to talk about three
simple design principles that

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:41.640
might help your Shiny apps look
and feel a little bit better,

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:44.490
as you're building them. And
also show some of the new work

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:48.240
that we've just begun
to help your apps look

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.420
and feel a little bit
better automatically.

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:51.920
All right.

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:55.440
So what's it like to
be a graphic designer?

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:56.580
I hate it.

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:58.060
I seriously hate it.

00:01:58.060 --> 00:01:59.188
I HATE IT.

00:01:59.188 --> 00:02:00.730
You guys know what
I'm talking about.

00:02:00.730 --> 00:02:03.610
Like you're looking at a thing
and it's just not working.

00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:05.350
It is horrible sometimes.

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:07.530
I mean, don't get me
wrong, 99% of the time,

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:09.419
it's like the greatest
thing I could possibly

00:02:09.419 --> 00:02:10.336
be doing with my time.

00:02:10.336 --> 00:02:12.360
It's the joy of
my life to do it.

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:13.860
But then when you
get to that thing,

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:14.980
you guys know what
I'm talking about.

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:17.700
Like you have that thing, it
just doesn't look or feel right

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:20.658
and you start to meddle with
some things to try and fix it.

00:02:20.658 --> 00:02:22.950
And there's a million different
directions you could go

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:24.370
and none of them are working.

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:27.630
And then it's just
like AAAAAHHHH, I hate it.

00:02:27.630 --> 00:02:30.732
So I just bring
that up to say that,

00:02:30.732 --> 00:02:32.940
I understand what you're
going through sometimes when

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:35.190
you're making your apps and
you're trying to make them

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:36.540
look good, we totally get it.

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:38.660
And, we're working on it.

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:41.160
We're trying to make this world
a little bit easier for you.

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:43.500
Here's here's how
I think of design.

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:46.690
Design as a form of
communication, communicating

00:02:46.690 --> 00:02:51.040
well, is really
difficult. Constructing

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:53.570
the perfect message
is a lot of work.

00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:56.000
And then if you add the
complexity of saying it

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:59.920
in a way that your audience will
hear, or even pay attention to,

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:03.100
it can really feel
like nearly impossible.

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:08.170
So putting an app
in front of somebody

00:03:08.170 --> 00:03:10.840
in my mind, all the work
that you guys are doing,

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:13.060
it's like having a
conversation with them.

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:16.540
And that's why I'm here to talk
about how designing for people

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:18.300
is hard.

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:21.690
Maybe, you tell me,
maybe 10% of the work

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:24.060
that you're doing when you're
working on a Shiny app,

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:26.050
is frontend work.

00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:29.920
And yet you're supposed to have
this rock solid data and also

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:33.820
an amazing UI and UX
experience for your users.

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:36.100
That's a really big ask.

00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:38.320
So our goal here
is for you to be

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:42.440
able to make better and
more beautiful apps.

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:45.510
And maybe have that even
take up less of your time.

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:48.570
That's kind of what we're
trying to work towards.

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:51.528
So when I got to the Shiny team,

00:03:51.528 --> 00:03:53.070
they asked me one
question, how can we

00:03:53.070 --> 00:03:54.630
make Shiny apps look better?

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:59.970
Side question, please tell us it
doesn't involve HTML and CSS.

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:01.830
Historically, the
benefit of Shiny

00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:04.020
has been can make something
decent without knowing

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:05.020
HTML and CSS.

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:07.230
But admittedly, as
Joe said yesterday,

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:10.590
there have been gaps in
improving this default look.

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:12.090
Joe recognized a
long list of people

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:14.100
in the community who
have stood in this gap,

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:16.920
like David Granjon
and his work on bs4dash.

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:19.500
Without the work of David
and how many other people

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:22.230
were on that list, it
was like 1,000,006,

00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:24.840
the apps would still look
like they were 10 years old,

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:26.500
probably.

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:29.290
Right now without
adding on some things,

00:04:29.290 --> 00:04:32.350
a fresh, Shiny app can
look a little stale.

00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:34.000
So where do we start?

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:37.010
Well, there's two things I
want to talk about today.

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:40.370
Look and functionality.

00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:42.590
Look as kind of its default
theme and functionality

00:04:42.590 --> 00:04:45.007
is the components that we're
serving up to you so that you

00:04:45.007 --> 00:04:47.543
can kind of present your data.

00:04:47.543 --> 00:04:48.460
Let's start with look.

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:49.010
Which means....

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:54.550
You need to have some real talk.

00:04:54.550 --> 00:04:58.600
Shiny uses Bootstrap 3
to render its UI

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:01.860
and Bootstrap 3
was created in 1852

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:04.420
(I don't know the exact date.)

00:05:04.420 --> 00:05:08.200
So Shiny looks stale because
Bootstrap 3, this year,

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:09.110
looks kind of stale.

00:05:09.110 --> 00:05:11.110
And this is not to speak
ill of Bootstrap 3.

00:05:11.110 --> 00:05:12.967
When it came out,
it was a miracle.

00:05:12.967 --> 00:05:14.800
But if you're going to
make a new Shiny app,

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:17.410
the best first step
you can take right now

00:05:17.410 --> 00:05:19.580
is to start with the
latest Bootstrap.

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:23.350
And right now that
is Bootstrap 5.

00:05:23.350 --> 00:05:27.520
Bootstrap 5 will make it look
much more contemporary right

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:28.290
out of the box.

00:05:28.290 --> 00:05:30.620
It has more components
for us to serve to you.

00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:33.400
You has better accessibility
tools, which is amazing.

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:34.900
And, of course, it
makes your app look

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:39.120
responsive and appropriate for
whatever size screen it's on.

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:40.300
So how do you do this?

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:41.770
You can easily do this.

00:05:41.770 --> 00:05:44.303
It's almost like... I was
looking at how they do this.

00:05:44.303 --> 00:05:45.720
It's literally
like somebody said,

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:47.860
"hey, Siri, make
this Bootstrap 5."

00:05:47.860 --> 00:05:50.160
That's kind of like what
they do, and they do it

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:53.725
with this R package
called bslib,

00:05:53.725 --> 00:05:55.850
by someone who could only be
described as the greatest

00:05:55.850 --> 00:05:56.933
human being on the planet.

00:05:56.933 --> 00:05:58.850
Carson Sievert, he
is really done...

00:05:58.850 --> 00:06:03.000
[STANDING OVATION, PEOPLE CRYING]
Yeah...

00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:06.150
This guy has done the Lord's work
for like 2.5 years to make

00:06:06.650 --> 00:06:08.150
this process easy for you.

00:06:08.150 --> 00:06:11.240
You might remember that Joe
Cheng mentioned bslib

00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:12.645
at the 2020 conference.

00:06:12.645 --> 00:06:14.270
He actually gave a
whole talk about it.

00:06:14.270 --> 00:06:17.630
So bslib provides tools
to easily customize Bootstrap

00:06:17.630 --> 00:06:20.360
and therefore your app's
appearance directly

00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:22.820
from R, allowing
you to quickly change

00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:24.380
the appearance of
your Shiny apps

00:06:24.380 --> 00:06:27.700
and for that matter,
R Markdown and Quarto stuff.

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:30.350
Today we're going to
talk just about Shiny.

00:06:30.350 --> 00:06:32.890
But it can't be
overstated how easy

00:06:32.890 --> 00:06:34.870
it is to change your
Shiny app's appearance,

00:06:34.870 --> 00:06:37.060
to match your
organization's branding

00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:39.020
with just a few lines of code.

00:06:39.020 --> 00:06:40.280
This is where I come from.

00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:43.900
We need this thing, but it
has to have our branding.

00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:46.680
(There's some happy
bslib in there...)

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:47.853
This is where I come from.

00:06:47.853 --> 00:06:50.270
We need this, and we need it
to look like Johnson &amp;amp; Johnson.

00:06:50.270 --> 00:06:51.030
And it's kind of
like, all right, well,

00:06:51.030 --> 00:06:51.970
I'm going to look at the red.

00:06:51.970 --> 00:06:52.680
We've got to make it red.

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:53.972
We got to look at the typeface.

00:06:53.972 --> 00:06:57.270
There's a couple of
key Sass variables

00:06:57.270 --> 00:06:59.550
that you can target
to really kind change

00:06:59.550 --> 00:07:00.910
the look of your entire thing.

00:07:00.910 --> 00:07:03.690
bslib can make it
very simple with one or two

00:07:03.690 --> 00:07:06.520
lines of code or it gives
you access to all, what is it,

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:12.550
1,400 different Sass variables
to change your Bootstrap theme.

00:07:12.550 --> 00:07:14.320
And so you can go
as deep as you want.

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:17.110
And this should be in kind of
a scalable, reproducible way.

00:07:17.110 --> 00:07:17.760
Right?

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:22.770
So bslib is unbelievable, OK.

00:07:22.770 --> 00:07:26.130
So that's look, you're
off to a great start.

00:07:26.130 --> 00:07:28.850
If you update your bootstrap
to Bootstrap 5 with bslib,

00:07:28.850 --> 00:07:30.600
maybe you're putting
in a couple of colors

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:33.127
to match your
organization's branding.

00:07:33.127 --> 00:07:34.710
Let's move on to
functionality, and get

00:07:34.710 --> 00:07:37.530
to some design principles
that occurred to me while I

00:07:37.530 --> 00:07:39.390
was looking through
any Shiny app

00:07:39.390 --> 00:07:42.370
I could get my hands on in
the last couple of months.

00:07:42.370 --> 00:07:43.870
I've been poring
through all of them

00:07:43.870 --> 00:07:47.960
from the simplest ones to
some of them are behemoths...

00:07:47.960 --> 00:07:49.720
They're crazy, they're nuts.

00:07:49.720 --> 00:07:51.650
But everything in
there is important.

00:07:51.650 --> 00:07:52.840
You can't judge it.

00:07:52.840 --> 00:07:55.960
And a few different design principles

00:07:55.960 --> 00:07:57.320
came to mind while
I looked at them.

00:07:57.320 --> 00:07:59.560
And when I was discussing
with those with the team,

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:02.110
we started coming up with
new functionality in reaction

00:08:02.110 --> 00:08:03.290
to those design principles.

00:08:03.290 --> 00:08:04.990
So let's get started.

00:08:04.990 --> 00:08:09.950
The first one comes from the
Gestalt Principles of Design.

00:08:09.950 --> 00:08:12.160
Now, whether designers
know it or not,

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:15.250
they're building things with
these principles in mind

00:08:15.250 --> 00:08:16.468
or as the foundation.

00:08:16.468 --> 00:08:18.010
If you're following
them, people will

00:08:18.010 --> 00:08:20.330
be able to easily
look at your app.

00:08:20.330 --> 00:08:22.240
If you're breaking
them, you kind of

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:24.160
don't want to look
at the app anymore.

00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:26.950
Every designer is using this,
whether they know it or not.

00:08:26.950 --> 00:08:29.290
So the first one
well, actually, I

00:08:29.290 --> 00:08:32.350
wanted to say that
Gestalt psychology is

00:08:32.350 --> 00:08:36.429
about 100 years old,
and it is all about how

00:08:36.429 --> 00:08:40.085
we process visual information.

00:08:40.085 --> 00:08:42.460
When you look at something,
it turns out you kind of look

00:08:42.460 --> 00:08:43.820
at the whole of something.

00:08:43.820 --> 00:08:46.275
You don't look at
the parts of it.

00:08:46.275 --> 00:08:47.650
Like when you look
at a bike, you

00:08:47.650 --> 00:08:51.240
don't go, well, look at that
little pedal right there.

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:52.490
Well, there's two pedals here.

00:08:52.490 --> 00:08:53.257
Oh, my gosh.

00:08:53.257 --> 00:08:54.590
There's wheels right next to it.

00:08:54.590 --> 00:08:55.310
What's going on here?

00:08:55.310 --> 00:08:56.720
And like hand like,
you don't do that.

00:08:56.720 --> 00:08:58.428
You look at it and
you go, that's a bike.

00:08:58.428 --> 00:09:00.820
And if you want to specific
things about the bike,

00:09:00.820 --> 00:09:02.320
you can then zero in.

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:03.620
Same thing with web design.

00:09:03.620 --> 00:09:05.290
You want that kind
of whole thing

00:09:05.290 --> 00:09:08.230
to immediately be something
that they want to dive into.

00:09:08.230 --> 00:09:11.320
So the first principle that
I want to talk about here

00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:14.920
is proximity: Users will
mentally group together

00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:18.177
objects that are spatially
close to each other.

00:09:18.177 --> 00:09:20.010
Now they're going to
do this no matter what.

00:09:20.010 --> 00:09:22.160
So if you have information
on one side of your screen

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:23.618
that's discussing
something, that's

00:09:23.618 --> 00:09:25.050
on the other side
of your screen,

00:09:25.050 --> 00:09:27.050
they're probably going
to group that information

00:09:27.050 --> 00:09:28.080
with something else over here.

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:29.120
And then realize, well,
that's not quite right,

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:30.650
and they're going to start
fishing around for what

00:09:30.650 --> 00:09:31.817
they should group that with.

00:09:31.817 --> 00:09:34.990
It's really better if you
just keep things in proximity.

00:09:34.990 --> 00:09:37.460
You know, like one of
the most popular UX

00:09:37.460 --> 00:09:41.615
books over the last 22 years
is called Don't Make Me Think.

00:09:41.615 --> 00:09:43.490
We're really trying to
serve this up in a way

00:09:43.490 --> 00:09:45.820
so that they don't have
to, like, process too much.

00:09:45.820 --> 00:09:47.653
They can just engage
with what you're trying

00:09:47.653 --> 00:09:49.240
to show them in your app.

00:09:49.240 --> 00:09:52.580
So what I want to do is
kind of show you some things

00:09:52.580 --> 00:09:53.210
that I saw.

00:09:53.210 --> 00:09:54.710
I saw this quite a
bit and just kind

00:09:54.710 --> 00:09:57.140
of give you an example
of what proximity means.

00:09:57.140 --> 00:09:58.160
Right here.

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:01.325
We've got a paragraph on the
left side and the sidebar.

00:10:01.325 --> 00:10:02.700
And it's speaking
about something

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:04.800
in this kind of right
part of the screen.

00:10:04.800 --> 00:10:07.050
In a perfect world, those
would be closer in proximity

00:10:07.050 --> 00:10:09.030
and that thing wouldn't
be in the sidebar

00:10:09.030 --> 00:10:11.040
that language would
be over near the thing

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:12.880
that it's referencing.

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:13.690
Also saw this.

00:10:13.690 --> 00:10:15.390
And this isn't
horrible, but this

00:10:15.390 --> 00:10:17.890
is a title that kind of says
this, that and the other thing.

00:10:17.890 --> 00:10:19.515
And it's talking
about the three things

00:10:19.515 --> 00:10:22.200
underneath that are about
this, that and the other thing.

00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:23.170
It's not horrible.

00:10:23.170 --> 00:10:24.630
It's kind of nice
serving up what's

00:10:24.630 --> 00:10:26.880
coming below on a platter,
but it would be really nice

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:28.817
if this and that
and the other thing

00:10:28.817 --> 00:10:30.900
were more closely in
proximity with the thing they

00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:33.368
were actually referencing.

00:10:33.368 --> 00:10:34.910
And I'm also seeing
this quite a bit.

00:10:34.910 --> 00:10:37.630
There are controls that are
kind of somewhere on your screen

00:10:37.630 --> 00:10:39.730
that control somewhere
else on your screen, inputs

00:10:39.730 --> 00:10:40.460
and outputs, right?

00:10:40.460 --> 00:10:41.835
And so I've
seen them where

00:10:41.835 --> 00:10:44.043
you have to start
messing with them

00:10:44.043 --> 00:10:45.650
to see what they control.

00:10:45.650 --> 00:10:48.568
It'd be better if those
controls were closer to what

00:10:48.568 --> 00:10:49.610
they actually controlled.

00:10:49.610 --> 00:10:51.940
So let's clean
this up real quick.

00:10:51.940 --> 00:10:54.270
We'll move this
paragraph over here.

00:10:54.270 --> 00:10:57.450
This, that and the other thing.

00:10:57.450 --> 00:10:59.200
And this really just
says, like, we update

00:10:59.200 --> 00:11:00.970
this on Wednesday mornings.

00:11:00.970 --> 00:11:03.670
Let's clean that
up a little bit.

00:11:03.670 --> 00:11:06.580
Let's put the controls
actually where they need

00:11:06.580 --> 00:11:08.728
to be to control something.

00:11:08.728 --> 00:11:10.520
It's starting to look
a little bit cleaner, right?

00:11:10.520 --> 00:11:13.420
We're causing the user
to think less before they

00:11:13.420 --> 00:11:16.170
engage with this thing.

00:11:16.170 --> 00:11:18.540
But, do you notice how none
of these cards on the screen

00:11:18.540 --> 00:11:20.400
are the same height?

00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:23.780
Or in some cases, not
even the same width.

00:11:23.780 --> 00:11:25.580
Don't you wish they
were kind of sized

00:11:25.580 --> 00:11:28.910
like this, where the gap in
between, the gutter in between

00:11:28.910 --> 00:11:30.170
was the same?

00:11:30.170 --> 00:11:32.193
There's actually a design
principle behind this.

00:11:32.193 --> 00:11:34.610
Maybe you didn't notice it,
but now that I pointed it out,

00:11:34.610 --> 00:11:37.240
did it kind of make
your skin crawl?

00:11:37.240 --> 00:11:38.670
This is called order:

00:11:38.670 --> 00:11:40.960
Things that are aligned
and symmetrical are more

00:11:40.960 --> 00:11:42.520
easily processed.

00:11:42.520 --> 00:11:44.950
When a person opens up your app.

00:11:44.950 --> 00:11:45.700
You know what?

00:11:45.700 --> 00:11:47.740
Let's make them not
think as much as we can

00:11:47.740 --> 00:11:51.520
and let them really dive into
the meat and potatoes

00:11:51.520 --> 00:11:52.747
of what you're trying to do.

00:11:52.747 --> 00:11:54.080
What you're trying to tell them.

00:11:54.080 --> 00:11:55.610
All right, so let's
clean this up.

00:11:55.610 --> 00:11:58.010
Got a bunch of things here.

00:11:58.010 --> 00:11:58.530
All right.

00:11:58.530 --> 00:12:01.030
This is starting to look
a little bit better.

00:12:01.030 --> 00:12:02.030
With proximity and

00:12:02.030 --> 00:12:03.550
order, your users
spend less time

00:12:03.550 --> 00:12:05.650
trying to figure out
how your thing works

00:12:05.650 --> 00:12:08.000
and more time using it the
way you intend them to.

00:12:10.248 --> 00:12:12.540
But really, I've just kind
of been moving stuff around,

00:12:12.540 --> 00:12:13.150
right?

00:12:13.150 --> 00:12:14.490
These are nice things to
keep in mind while you're

00:12:14.490 --> 00:12:16.590
building your app, but your
mind is really in other places

00:12:16.590 --> 00:12:19.007
while you're writing your
R code and putting

00:12:19.007 --> 00:12:20.172
together your data, right?

00:12:20.172 --> 00:12:21.880
So we were trying to
figure out, is there

00:12:21.880 --> 00:12:24.220
something here that we
can put in the process

00:12:24.220 --> 00:12:26.510
so that it can kind of
put up some guardrails?

00:12:26.510 --> 00:12:28.210
So while you're
building these apps,

00:12:28.210 --> 00:12:29.590
you'll kind of stay
in the lane and you

00:12:29.590 --> 00:12:31.673
don't have to think about
proximity because you're

00:12:31.673 --> 00:12:35.223
kind of just building everything
in proximity automatically.

00:12:35.223 --> 00:12:37.390
We realize this was the
first piece of functionality

00:12:37.390 --> 00:12:39.020
we should address.

00:12:39.020 --> 00:12:41.570
That's a card.

00:12:41.570 --> 00:12:44.520
A lot of cards oftentimes
become a dashboard.

00:12:44.520 --> 00:12:47.330
And from what I can tell,
everybody in this room

00:12:47.330 --> 00:12:49.260
is like the Oprah of dashboards.

00:12:49.260 --> 00:12:50.990
You're just like,
you get a dashboard,

00:12:50.990 --> 00:12:53.400
you'll get a dashboard,
look on your seat.

00:12:53.400 --> 00:12:55.580
We all have a dashboard.

00:12:55.580 --> 00:12:58.080
So a lot of
dashboards everywhere.

00:12:58.080 --> 00:12:59.067
And I get it.

00:12:59.067 --> 00:13:00.150
You know how to make them.

00:13:00.150 --> 00:13:01.920
Your users know how
to consume them.

00:13:01.920 --> 00:13:05.000
Everybody wins.

00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:07.680
Bootstrap 3
didn't have cards.

00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:08.460
Guess what?

00:13:08.460 --> 00:13:11.470
Bootstrap 5 does, so...

00:13:11.470 --> 00:13:15.910
We decided cards should become
a first class citizen of Shiny

00:13:15.910 --> 00:13:19.660
within bslib and that they
should look good automatically,

00:13:19.660 --> 00:13:21.420
so...

00:13:21.420 --> 00:13:24.810
We decided this,
and then we didn't

00:13:24.810 --> 00:13:27.760
hear from Joe Cheng
for a day or two.

00:13:27.760 --> 00:13:30.700
And he came back with, of
course a fully-fledged card API

00:13:30.700 --> 00:13:32.050
for us to put in bslib.

00:13:32.050 --> 00:13:37.080
And this is what they look by
default. Pretty simple stuff.

00:13:37.080 --> 00:13:39.880
You get a card header
for a title, this, that,

00:13:39.880 --> 00:13:41.720
or the other thing.

00:13:41.720 --> 00:13:44.787
You have a main area
for whatever you want.

00:13:44.787 --> 00:13:47.120
It can be an image, it can
be a plot, can be some prose,

00:13:47.120 --> 00:13:48.830
a button, whatever
you can think of,

00:13:48.830 --> 00:13:49.913
you can throw it in there.

00:13:49.913 --> 00:13:51.770
And if there's any
tertiary information,

00:13:51.770 --> 00:13:53.970
like we updated
this the other day...

00:13:53.970 --> 00:13:54.940
Throw that at the bottom.

00:13:54.940 --> 00:13:59.640
This does a lot of the design,
the proximity  the rder

00:13:59.640 --> 00:14:01.945
heavy lifting for you.

00:14:01.945 --> 00:14:02.570
Check this out.

00:14:02.570 --> 00:14:04.000
Here's a row of
cards by example.

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:05.910
Here's what it would look like.

00:14:05.910 --> 00:14:10.493
No matter what you put in them,
they will be the same height.

00:14:10.493 --> 00:14:11.410
Because you know what?

00:14:11.410 --> 00:14:12.630
We're all grown ups in
this room and maybe we

00:14:12.630 --> 00:14:13.505
can have nice things.

00:14:16.820 --> 00:14:20.180
Something Bootstrap doesn't
offer but we think

00:14:20.180 --> 00:14:22.220
is really important
is sometimes you

00:14:22.220 --> 00:14:24.740
want to be able to tweak the
parameters of what is inside

00:14:24.740 --> 00:14:27.230
the card or maybe even
just kind of change

00:14:27.230 --> 00:14:29.540
wholesale, what's
showing in this card.

00:14:29.540 --> 00:14:33.110
And so you guys are very
used to using tabs and menus

00:14:33.110 --> 00:14:33.960
and dropdowns.

00:14:33.960 --> 00:14:36.692
And so when you put that same
code within this card context,

00:14:36.692 --> 00:14:38.150
it will look like
this and give you

00:14:38.150 --> 00:14:40.410
the functionality of changing
what's inside the card.

00:14:43.100 --> 00:14:45.500
If you want to make
dashboards right now,

00:14:45.500 --> 00:14:47.370
you're probably using flexdashboard,

00:14:47.370 --> 00:14:48.390
and why wouldn't you?

00:14:48.390 --> 00:14:50.570
So simple, so
clean... For a lot of you,

00:14:50.570 --> 00:14:51.830
that's all you need.

00:14:51.830 --> 00:14:55.800
If you want a little bit
more functionality,

00:14:55.800 --> 00:14:57.540
you're probably
using Shiny Dashboard

00:14:57.540 --> 00:15:00.390
or maybe you're using bs4dash.

00:15:00.390 --> 00:15:05.680
We're building out in
bslib, on top of Bootstrap 5,

00:15:05.680 --> 00:15:08.280
A new UI toolkit for bslib.

00:15:08.280 --> 00:15:11.390
And once we're done, you'll be
able to use its elements

00:15:11.390 --> 00:15:11.890
à la carte.

00:15:11.890 --> 00:15:14.040
You'll be able to kind
of grab what you need

00:15:14.040 --> 00:15:16.180
and not grab the rest.

00:15:16.180 --> 00:15:19.560
Basically, you won't have to
import admin LTE to just put

00:15:19.560 --> 00:15:21.693
a box around something.

00:15:21.693 --> 00:15:23.610
The things you love about
those other packages

00:15:23.610 --> 00:15:25.920
will be available in
bslib and therefore, you'll

00:15:25.920 --> 00:15:29.430
be able to use them in
Shiny, R Markdown, Quarto.

00:15:29.430 --> 00:15:32.430
This is more of, Joe called
it a library approach,

00:15:32.430 --> 00:15:33.630
more of a library approach.

00:15:33.630 --> 00:15:35.460
Grab what you need
just right there

00:15:35.460 --> 00:15:37.300
and hopefully the
design will look good.

00:15:37.300 --> 00:15:39.550
If you need to change some
of the branding, go for it.

00:15:39.550 --> 00:15:42.400
That's pretty easy, too.

00:15:42.400 --> 00:15:44.140
Not everything needs
to be a dashboard,

00:15:44.140 --> 00:15:45.932
but bringing in a little
dashboard elements

00:15:45.932 --> 00:15:49.765
now and then to your app can
be a pretty powerful thing.

00:15:49.765 --> 00:15:51.140
Pretty good thing
for your users.

00:15:51.140 --> 00:15:54.635
So let's talk about your users.

00:15:54.635 --> 00:15:56.260
last thing I want to
talk about is just

00:15:56.260 --> 00:15:58.480
information architecture,
not from Gestalt,

00:15:58.480 --> 00:16:01.180
but it's when you
look at your app,

00:16:01.180 --> 00:16:04.780
let's say you've narrowed down
all of the content to as little

00:16:04.780 --> 00:16:08.590
as it can be, and it's
still kind of a mess.

00:16:08.590 --> 00:16:10.450
It's still kind of
hard to engage with.

00:16:10.450 --> 00:16:12.610
This is where you can
start kind of prioritizing

00:16:12.610 --> 00:16:14.560
what you show based on
the users that you're

00:16:14.560 --> 00:16:17.680
going to be presenting
it to, any app you make,

00:16:17.680 --> 00:16:20.500
any website you make,
any poster you make,

00:16:20.500 --> 00:16:23.763
you could probably prioritize
who is going to engage with it.

00:16:23.763 --> 00:16:25.180
You can come up
with three or four

00:16:25.180 --> 00:16:27.520
different, three or
five different users.

00:16:27.520 --> 00:16:29.800
A lot of people
call them personas.

00:16:29.800 --> 00:16:34.330
So let's prioritize based on
who's going to be using it

00:16:34.330 --> 00:16:36.440
and just show them
what they need to know.

00:16:36.440 --> 00:16:38.710
The way that's
easiest to prioritize

00:16:38.710 --> 00:16:42.160
for your different users is
by how much time of theirs you

00:16:42.160 --> 00:16:42.800
have.

00:16:42.800 --> 00:16:44.650
Some people are going to be
looking at your dashboards,

00:16:44.650 --> 00:16:46.700
maybe like a boss is
looking at a dashboard.

00:16:46.700 --> 00:16:50.165
They have 30 seconds to
get the main headlines

00:16:50.165 --> 00:16:51.790
and then they're
moving on to check out

00:16:51.790 --> 00:16:54.880
like 15 other dashboards
of 15 other departments.

00:16:54.880 --> 00:16:56.680
Maybe there's a
department leader

00:16:56.680 --> 00:16:59.230
that has a couple of minutes,
a couple of times a week.

00:16:59.230 --> 00:17:00.490
They probably need
to know everything

00:17:00.490 --> 00:17:01.610
that the boss needs to know.

00:17:01.610 --> 00:17:03.530
They probably also want
to a little bit more.

00:17:03.530 --> 00:17:05.050
So that stuff that
they need to know

00:17:05.050 --> 00:17:07.869
if it's too messy
initially to kind of put

00:17:07.869 --> 00:17:09.758
that away behind the click.

00:17:09.758 --> 00:17:11.300
There's probably a
third person who's

00:17:11.300 --> 00:17:14.900
a specialist might spend all
week, every week in your app

00:17:14.900 --> 00:17:17.865
and need to respond in real
time to what's happening there.

00:17:17.865 --> 00:17:19.490
They might need to
really kind of drill

00:17:19.490 --> 00:17:23.770
down and see some very specific
things that are happening.

00:17:23.770 --> 00:17:26.550
And so that is OK for them
to click behind the thing.

00:17:26.550 --> 00:17:28.300
So what I want to do
is I want to show you

00:17:28.300 --> 00:17:31.785
what bslib looks
like in a live example

00:17:31.785 --> 00:17:33.410
here, the stuff that
we've been making.

00:17:33.410 --> 00:17:36.540
So we've got this sidebar here.

00:17:36.540 --> 00:17:40.860
And we have decided that we
had too much in our sidebar.

00:17:40.860 --> 00:17:44.050
So we've categorized them into
five different accordion menus.

00:17:44.050 --> 00:17:45.510
We're bringing in accordions.

00:17:45.510 --> 00:17:47.310
You can have them open
or closed because we

00:17:47.310 --> 00:17:49.810
think the boss would want to
engage with these first two.

00:17:49.810 --> 00:17:51.780
We open up those and
we close the rest.

00:17:51.780 --> 00:17:53.850
Accordions are going to be a
thing that you can kind of put

00:17:53.850 --> 00:17:54.670
in wherever you want.

00:17:54.670 --> 00:17:56.087
Doesn't have to
be in the sidebar.

00:17:58.510 --> 00:17:59.240
All right.

00:17:59.240 --> 00:18:02.740
So now we're going to engage
with this a little bit.

00:18:02.740 --> 00:18:05.250
Let's see.

00:18:05.250 --> 00:18:08.430
We're going to bring in button
groups from Bootstrap 5

00:18:08.430 --> 00:18:12.640
that can also act as
checkboxes or radios.

00:18:12.640 --> 00:18:15.857
So in this app, there's
three different airports.

00:18:15.857 --> 00:18:17.440
Let's see, where do
we want to fly to?

00:18:17.440 --> 00:18:19.260
I obviously want
to fly to Hawaii.

00:18:19.260 --> 00:18:21.100
And I don't know
where Bob Hope is.

00:18:21.100 --> 00:18:22.000
Is that Palm Springs?

00:18:22.000 --> 00:18:22.667
I like Bob Hope.

00:18:22.667 --> 00:18:24.220
So we'll do that.

00:18:24.220 --> 00:18:24.720
All right.

00:18:24.720 --> 00:18:31.500
So just for fun, let's
close these because.

00:18:31.500 --> 00:18:32.230
That's fun.

00:18:32.230 --> 00:18:33.900
Here's another thing
we can do where

00:18:33.900 --> 00:18:36.900
we're working on some
histograms that show with range

00:18:36.900 --> 00:18:40.200
sliders, so we bring some more
meaning to a ranch slider,

00:18:40.200 --> 00:18:43.560
let people kind of investigate
the data little bit that way.

00:18:43.560 --> 00:18:46.420
And this is all kind
of changing

00:18:46.420 --> 00:18:49.217
what's showing on the right.

00:18:49.217 --> 00:18:50.550
Now, let's move on to the cards here.

00:18:53.240 --> 00:18:54.420
Cards are amazing.

00:18:54.420 --> 00:18:58.100
Sometimes people put a
lot of cards on a page.

00:18:58.100 --> 00:18:59.880
It can get to be a lot.

00:18:59.880 --> 00:19:03.020
My rule of thumb is if
you reach seven cards, maybe

00:19:03.020 --> 00:19:06.560
it's time to start looking
at making a new page.

00:19:06.560 --> 00:19:08.600
But in this case, we've
got this globe here

00:19:08.600 --> 00:19:10.640
and it's kind of
showing some data

00:19:10.640 --> 00:19:14.010
and it's a little hard to
make out what it's doing,

00:19:14.010 --> 00:19:16.580
so we've added a
full screen feature

00:19:16.580 --> 00:19:21.080
to make a card go full screen.

00:19:21.080 --> 00:19:26.190
And so now you can play
with a bit more real estate.

00:19:26.190 --> 00:19:27.640
You can zoom in if you want.

00:19:31.850 --> 00:19:32.840
Let's see.

00:19:32.840 --> 00:19:36.970
Oh well.

00:19:36.970 --> 00:19:38.200
That card to the right.

00:19:38.200 --> 00:19:44.083
I zoomed in and I was able to
see a bit more information.

00:19:44.083 --> 00:19:45.250
About to get the light here.

00:19:45.250 --> 00:19:46.890
So I want to talk
about I want to talk

00:19:46.890 --> 00:19:48.777
about value boxes real quick.

00:19:48.777 --> 00:19:50.110
We're going to make value boxes.

00:19:50.110 --> 00:19:51.680
You can put an icon
up in the corner.

00:19:51.680 --> 00:19:53.230
It's just kind of an HTML space.

00:19:53.230 --> 00:19:55.563
I was sitting next to the
dude who made plot, so I said,

00:19:55.563 --> 00:19:57.490
can we have a
sparkline for an icon?

00:19:57.490 --> 00:19:58.470
He said, sure.

00:19:58.470 --> 00:20:01.740
This is great for the boss
who is looking at this,

00:20:01.740 --> 00:20:06.660
but maybe you want to be able
to click in and see a bit more.

00:20:06.660 --> 00:20:09.400
So value boxes can
be full screen.

00:20:09.400 --> 00:20:10.930
And you can see
more information.

00:20:10.930 --> 00:20:13.472
I don't know if you noticed,
but now there's an x and y-axis,

00:20:13.472 --> 00:20:15.090
you can show more stuff.

00:20:15.090 --> 00:20:15.700
All right.

00:20:15.700 --> 00:20:17.560
I'm about to get the gong here.

00:20:17.560 --> 00:20:20.700
So let me just say, I think
this will be especially powerful

00:20:20.700 --> 00:20:24.050
when it comes to tables.

00:20:24.050 --> 00:20:26.010
All of this comes from
conversations with you.

00:20:26.010 --> 00:20:27.530
We'd love to hear
from you and find out

00:20:27.530 --> 00:20:29.780
what you're struggling with
and how we can maybe help.

00:20:29.780 --> 00:20:30.830
So reach out.

00:20:30.830 --> 00:20:35.170
My email is greg at rstudio.com,
and I'd love to hang out.

